{% sec 1, "Follow One Pound", "Where does a saver's money actually go?", "Follow one pound" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The last note was a map of who's who. This one follows the money across that map, the way you'd follow a parcel from the sender to the doorstep.</p>
    <p>Take a teacher. Every month, part of her salary goes into her pension. Here's one realistic journey for that money:</p>
  </div>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>The teacher</strong> pays into her pension fund every month.</li>
    <li><strong>The pension fund</strong> (an asset owner) decides how to split its money: so much in shares, so much in bonds, a bit in hedge funds and private equity. It often pays consultants to help it decide.</li>
    <li>It hands a slice to <strong>a hedge fund</strong> (an asset manager), becoming one of its limited partners.</li>
    <li>The hedge fund opens an account with <strong>a prime broker</strong> (a bank), which lends it extra money so it can invest more than it was given.</li>
    <li>A hedge fund analyst finds a company they like, helped by <strong>sell-side research</strong> and a meeting with the company's bosses that a broker arranged.</li>
    <li>The fund buys the shares on <strong>a stock exchange</strong> through a broker, and the prime broker settles the trade and holds the shares safely.</li>
    <li>Those shares only exist because, a few years earlier, <strong>an investment bank</strong> took the company public in an IPO.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Then it goes back the other way. If the investment does well, the profit flows back up to the hedge fund, then to the pension fund, and eventually becomes the teacher's pension. So the system is a circle, not a one-way street.</p>
    <p>Every firm on that journey does a real job, and every one of them takes a slice. Finance calls this {% gl "fee layering" %}fee layering{% endgl %}, and it's the reason big savers spend so much time arguing about costs.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Follow a pound: where the gain goes</h3><p>The teacher's \(\text{£}1{,}000\) goes into her pension for a year. Pick how the pension invests it, and how the market does. The bars show where each slice of the gain ends up. The fees are typical examples, not any real fund's.</p></div>
